<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milcom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Milkom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Milcom, or Milkom, was the national god of the Ammonites, worshipped in their kingdom east of the Jordan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. His name is related to the Semitic word for 'king', and he appears in Ammonite inscriptions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman-citadel-inscription">Amman Citadel Inscription</a>. The Bible records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> built a shrine to him near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, later destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milcom.jpg" alt="Milcom" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Makeandtoss · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_an_Ammonite_deified_King_on_display_at_the_Jordan_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Iron Age, as Egyptian power over Canaan declined, peoples and cities formed regional and national cults round a patron god: Chemosh of the Moabites, <b>Milcom</b> of the Ammonites, Qos of the Edomites, Yahweh of the Israelites, Melqart of Tyre and Eshmun of Sidon.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Ammonite kings ruled a farming and herding land guarded by watchtowers, under the national god <b>Milkom</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ammon">Ammon</a></li></ul></section>
